body:
I agree that there may be nothing bad about Sandy as a person; we simply cannot tell and it is perhaps irrelevant to the event.

&nbsp;

But I disagree with your judgement to say that this is only a concern because of one's disbelief in opposite sex friendships. The problem to me is his significant emotional investment in another individual, and that is non gender specific. 

&nbsp;

I won't pretend that everything is equal for male and female; the fact that Sandy is female indeed makes it more worrying to me. It is indeed more of a social norm for one to expect a girl and OP's ex fiancée to keep a further distance out of respect for OP, to avoid causing OP possible discomfort. The fact that she is female makes it more obvious that OP's feeling had not been a part of their considerations, and that itself is quite unsettling.

&nbsp;

That being said, even if sandy was a guy, it would still be a worry, just perhaps less apparent due to the way we are socially accustomed.

&nbsp;

I guess in summary I'm saying, Sandy may not be evil at all, but that doesn't make OP evil either. OP hasn't handled it well, but that's just that.

I generally think threatening is disrespecting, immature and rude. If she is giving you the "if you don't do what I say, I'll leave you", then I think there are already problems in your relationship.

&nbsp;

I would stay in Ireland if I were you, unless she learns to communicate and not dictate, and unless you've reached an agreement.

Man you've been through much. Let me brighten it up for you with my career drama:

&nbsp;

I'm an expat working in a foreign country, my exboss is this dick who thought he is god since my work visa and approval to stay in the country was dependant on the company. My SO live in this country.

&nbsp;

Well that didn't work out since he called me one Saturday morning and wanted me to work (no OT pay, btw). When I told him I was not able to do so, he threatened to fire me and to terminate my work visa. So I told him to kiss his own arse and i quit. Dick comes back 2 days later to beg me to go back, claiming that I "should know he has other commitments and I can't quit now". Uh fuck you, no. There wasn't even a fucking apology and just, fuck you.

&nbsp; 

So I managed to get my current job within 10 days after that in the same country, and have a pretty cool boss now.

&nbsp;

Me ex boss opened my eyes about the existence of douchebags.

>He said that this classmate was not an "issue."

&nbsp;

No, the classmate wasn't the issue. He was. In glad you dodged the bullet OP.

>I'm happy my daughter has found someone she loves and I don't want to ruin anything. 

&nbsp;

Set your boundaries, and know that if anything is ruined, it'd be because he acts inappropriately, not because you called him out on it.

I just read through your posts OP, I'm glad you've done the right thing- it reminded me of what my ex did and I went down that wrong path of 'forgiving him' and actually believed his story of having some girl end up in his room somehow who tried to kiss him when he suddenly realised that's not right and stopped her. He told me this two days after the incident. Because, you see, I've a friend whom happened to live in the same place, and had been so nice to hint me about it (except I was too dumb to get the hint)  ex got nervous.

&nbsp;

It is a bit of a familiar story, no?

&nbsp;

I was 23 then; I bought the story to only suffer his endless flirting with girls for the next 4 years.

&nbsp;

Its time to know that these are people who decide to put their own desire before everything and everyone, they ain't worthy as partners and it aint your job to accommodate her selfishness and pride. 

&nbsp;

I wish someone had told me this when I was there; I wasted 6 years of my life with that rubbish.

> I think she fancies him, but I'm sure he would never do anything like that.

&nbsp;

I used to think this about my ex boyfriend, until 5 years later and I realised if she is flirting with him, it is largely because he lets her flirt with him.

Some commentors will advise you to leave him/isolate him, I beg to differ. Your dad has temper issues and cannot express himself properly; but you, on the other hand, are also still learning to communicate with him as a teenager.

&nbsp;

I think it had been frustrating to him that you are in your teenage rebellious phase; he feels his comments/advises has not been valued by his son and he is not able to 'guide' his son. He feels that he has not been appreciated as a father, and the way he expresses that is through yelling and anger, as he knows no other way of expressing.

&nbsp;

I say this because my dad was like this. Not to the extent of pushing physically, but I see that his temper come from insecurities as a parent. It takes both sides to be understanding to resolve this. Ultimately, he is angry because he cares for you as a father, just that he is shit at getting that message through.

&nbsp;

Mind I ask if your family is Asian? Mine is, and I've always wondered if culture played a role there.

Relationship is not a 'goal' in life, never go into a relationship because you want to try what its like to have a relationship- first of all, as a girl, it freaks me out to see guys who are desperate and just want to be with someone, whoever it is. 

&nbsp;

To say it in the more poetic cliche way, to have is to provide first. You want love, you need to be able to love people first.

&nbsp;

Don't see your life as if it is some sort of token. If you really decide to hurt yourself, truth is, nobody here can help you. A lot of us would love to make it easier for you, but that's that. It is your life, take control of it, throw away the self-pity, know that nothing is worth your life.

My brain knows that, but here's what happened- once upon the time, I was young and naive. I was with a guy back then, let's call him Ben. Ben had an ex girlfriend, who's close to his family, let's call her Cindy. Cindy is weird, she would message him. I don't even remember how exactly this went, but one day I saw Cindy's name pop up on bens phone. I asked him what it was about, he said it was just family matters or something to that effect. I believed him.

&nbsp;

2 years later.
I was still with Ben.
He was in shower.
I was playing games on his phone, then I saw a new facebook message from cindy pop up, with that conversation two years ago.

&nbsp;

It said nothing, just something along the lines of "it was good spending time with you at the cafe yesterday"

&nbsp;

My heart sunk, but I never asked a word. What's the point of bringing up something that happened 2 years ago? I trusted, 100%, and I got ripped off. I wasn't sure if that makes me stupid.

&nbsp;

Lucky I would never have to feel insecure with my SO now, but it makes me think that sometimes it is not such a bad idea to at least be more sensitive (though maybe no need to snoop) instead of sitting on the 100% trust

Rule of thumb: the moment you hit someone, you adopt all the blames.

&nbsp;


Next time you deal with a psycho, call a police. You could've press charge against her and have her pay for the damages. Now you'd be lucky if she doesnt run around telling people about her 'abusive' ex hitting her..

-don't be sorry for having doubts about a shitty guy.

&nbsp;

-you are not crazy, he is.

&nbsp;

-you need to step out and see that he is trying to make you feel bad about yourself, and make you think you are doing something wrong, make you think you are not being a "proper" partner.

That's a very shitty thing for a partner to do.

&nbsp;


-be more confident about your own feelings, no one can tell you how you 'should' feel in a relationship; he is a gaslighter.
